<h2>The Examined Life</h2>

<div>
<p>Near the end of the <em>Apology</em>, Socrates is given the opportunity to propose his own punishment — an alternative that might have saved his life. He could offer exile. He could offer silence. Instead he refuses both and accepts death, because accepting either condition would have required him to stop philosophizing. His reason: <em>the unexamined life is not worth living</em>.</p>
<p>Whatever you think of Socrates — and it is reasonable to find him exasperating, admirable, or both — this is a remarkable thing to say in front of a jury that has just voted to convict you. It is not a rhetorical gesture. It is a philosophical claim about what makes a human life genuinely worthwhile, and Socrates is staking his life on it.</p>
<p>The two questions below approach this claim from different angles. Both connect to material across all three dialogues. <strong>Choose one and respond to it.</strong></p>
 
<div class="callout callout--concept">
  <span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span>
  <div class="callout__content">
    <p><strong>Question 1</strong></p>
    <p>"The unexamined life is not worth living." Is Socrates right?</p>
    <p>Socrates does not just preach this claim — he demonstrates it through his entire method. The elenchus forces people to examine beliefs they have never questioned. In the <em>Euthyphro</em>, Euthyphro has never seriously asked himself what piety actually is. In the <em>Crito</em>, Crito has never seriously asked himself whether popular opinion is a reliable guide to justice.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--note">
  <span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8505;</span>
  <div class="callout__content">
    <p><strong>Question 2</strong></p>
    <p>In the <em>Apology</em>, Socrates says he would disobey a court order to stop philosophizing. In the <em>Crito</em>, he refuses to escape an unjust execution. Are these two positions consistent?</p>
    <p>The tension is real. In one dialogue Socrates places his mission above the law. In the other he places the law above his survival. His argument in the <em>Crito</em> — that he has tacitly agreed to abide by Athens’ laws by living there his whole life — seems to bind him in a way that the <em>Apology</em>’s commitment to philosophy does not.</p>
    <p>Do you think these positions can be reconciled? If so, explain how — what principle or distinction makes both positions coherent at the same time? If not, identify which position you find more defensible and explain why. You may also draw on the Euthyphro dilemma if it helps: if morality has an independent standard, does that give Socrates grounds to disobey unjust laws regardless of what the <em>Crito</em> argument implies?</p>
  </div>
</div>
